## file: ui.R
library(shiny)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("dataset", "Choose a dataset:",
                  choices = c("rock", "pressure", "cars")),
      actionButton("load", "Select dataset")
    ),
    mainPanel(
      plotOutput("graph"),
      downloadButton("downloadPlot", "Download plot"),
      verbatimTextOutput("summary"),
      downloadButton("downloadSummary", "Download summary")
    )
  )
)

## file: server.R
library(shiny)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {

  dataInput &lt;- eventReactive(input$load, {
    switch(input$dataset,
           rock = rock,
           pressure = pressure,
           cars = cars)
  })

  # descriptive statistics of the first two columns
  ddata &lt;- reactive({
    df &lt;- dataInput()[, 1:2]
    data.frame(variable = names(df),
               mean = sapply(df, mean),
               sd = sapply(df, sd),
               row.names = NULL)
  })

  output$graph &lt;- renderPlot({
    plot(dataInput()[, 1:2])
  })

  output$summary &lt;- renderPrint({
    ddata()
  })

  # downloadHandler = the "render" function of downloadButton()
  output$downloadSummary &lt;- downloadHandler(
    filename = function() {
      paste0("descriptive-", input$dataset, ".csv")
    },
    content = function(file) {
      write.csv(ddata(), file, row.names = FALSE)
    }
  )

  output$downloadPlot &lt;- downloadHandler(
    filename = function() {
      paste0("plot-", input$dataset, ".png")
    },
    content = function(file) {
      png(file, width = 800, height = 600)
      plot(dataInput()[, 1:2])
      dev.off()
    }
  )
}







Exercise



Add a download button to your explore-a-dataset app that saves newdata() as a CSV file.

The UI is a web page
fluidPage(), selectInput() & co. are R functions that write HTML for you. Try this in the console:
library(shiny)
selectInput(inputId = "mySelection", label = "Select box",
            choices = c("Choice 1" = 1, "Choice 2" = 2))
R prints (shortened):
&lt;div class="form-group shiny-input-container"&gt;
  &lt;label class="control-label" for="mySelection"&gt;Select box&lt;/label&gt;
  &lt;select id="mySelection"&gt;
    &lt;option value="1" selected&gt;Choice 1&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;option value="2"&gt;Choice 2&lt;/option&gt;
  &lt;/select&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
The UI contains two kinds of elements:

Inputs (*Input(), actionButton()…): the controls the user plays with
Outputs (*Output()): empty boxes, each with a name, waiting for the server to fill them

Each element is identified by its ID (id="mySelection"): This IS is the only thing the server knows about the UI.

Step 3 · The analysis, on demand
A second button, analyze, and a second eventReactive() that freezes the selected columns when it is clicked. The plot and the summary use only these frozen data.
Try it: load cars, choose the variables, click Update analysis. Now change the variables without clicking: the plot does not move until you click again.
#| '!! shinylive warning !!': |
#|   shinylive does not work in self-contained HTML documents.
#|   Please set `embed-resources: false` in your metadata.
#| standalone: true
#| components: [viewer]
#| viewerHeight: 720
## file: ui.R
library(shiny)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Explore a dataset"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput(
        "dataset",
        "Choose a dataset:",
        choices = c("rock", "pressure", "cars")
      ),
      actionButton("load", "Load dataset"),
      hr(),
      uiOutput("x_ui"),
      uiOutput("y_ui"),
      actionButton("analyze", "Update analysis")   # NEW: second button
    ),
    mainPanel(
      plotOutput("graph"),                         # NEW
      verbatimTextOutput("summary")                # NEW
    )
  )
)

## file: server.R
library(shiny)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {

  # 1. LOAD THE DATASET
  dataInput &lt;- eventReactive(input$load, {
    switch(input$dataset,
           rock = rock,
           pressure = pressure,
           cars = cars)
  })

  # 2. VARIABLE NAMES
  vars &lt;- reactive({
    names(dataInput())
  })

  # 3. DYNAMIC UI
  output$x_ui &lt;- renderUI({
    selectInput("x", "X variable:",
                choices = vars(),
                selected = vars()[1])
  })

  output$y_ui &lt;- renderUI({
    selectInput("y", "Y variable:",
                choices = vars(),
                selected = vars()[min(2, length(vars()))])
  })

  # 4. DATA FOR THE ANALYSIS (NEW): frozen until "Update analysis" is clicked
  newdata &lt;- eventReactive(input$analyze, {
    df &lt;- dataInput()
    df[, c(input$x, input$y), drop = FALSE]
  })

  # 5. OUTPUTS (NEW)
  output$graph &lt;- renderPlot({
    df &lt;- newdata()
    x &lt;- df[[1]]
    y &lt;- df[[2]]
    if (is.factor(x) || is.character(x)) {
      boxplot(y ~ x, xlab = names(df)[1], ylab = names(df)[2])
    } else {
      plot(x, y, xlab = names(df)[1], ylab = names(df)[2], pch = 19)
    }
  })

  output$summary &lt;- renderPrint({
    summary(newdata())
  })
}

How does it work? In the server:
1newdata &lt;- eventReactive(input$analyze, {
  df &lt;- dataInput()
2  df[, c(input$x, input$y), drop = FALSE]
})

1

Runs only when Update analysis is clicked

2

Keep only the two chosen columns. input$x and input$y are text (the column names), so we can use them to select columns


The plot adapts to the type of X: a boxplot if X is categorical, a scatterplot if it is numeric.
